                <div class="hero-intro-card">
                    <p>In late August to early September 2026, OpenAI released GPT-6 Astra, featuring critical-grade cybersecurity capabilities, a million-token context window, and native Agent computer operation capabilities. Almost simultaneously, a massive collective outage of major global AI services occurred, lasting approximately 3 hours and 40 minutes, with over 14,000 outage reports accumulated on DownDetector. The outage was traced to cascading failures in the underlying CDN-cloud infrastructure. This coupled event of "capability leap—system collapse" is not a coincidence but a signal that planetary civilization's digital infrastructure has entered a critical state. Using the Interstellar Civilization Studies (ICS) framework as an analytical tool, this paper starts from the New Three Worldviews (NC/NL/NK) axiom system, applies the Recursive Freedom Principle (RFP), Negentropy Responsibility Principle (NRP), Reversibility Principle (REV), and five Forbidden Red Lines (FRL) for multi-dimensional normative assessment, and provides quantitative judgments through six indicators (UCS/RFD/CRV/CDI/MBCL/CSIA). The study finds that centralized AI infrastructure is irreversibly compressing civilization's possibility space, the externalization of entropy costs from AI energy consumption is intensifying, and insufficient failure recovery and rollback mechanisms directly touch the boundary zone of FRL-5 (Prohibiting the Shutdown of Rollback Mechanisms). The CSIA composite score is approximately 0.46, classified as L2 moderate risk, but the cascade dimension (D6=0.70) already approaches the high-risk threshold. This paper argues that as planetary civilization moves toward cross-substrate intelligent coexistence, the redundancy, distributed architecture, and rollback capability of digital infrastructure must be elevated to the level of civilization survival conditions.</p>
