                    <h2 class="en-section-title">I. Introduction</h2>
<div class="content-card">
<p>In late summer 2026, two events struck like two nails falling almost simultaneously, driven into the same spot.</p>
<p>One was OpenAI's release of GPT-6 Astra. A million-token context window means the machine can ingest an entire library at once; critical-grade cybersecurity capabilities mean it begins to touch the threshold of national-level offense and defense; native Agent computer operation capabilities mean it is no longer just a text engine answering questions, but a digital actor that can move the mouse, click buttons, and execute operations. The speed of capability boundary expansion has already outpaced the update cycles of most governance frameworks.</p>
<p>The other was the collective outage of major global AI services. Approximately 3 hours and 40 minutes, over 14,000 outage reports, hundreds of media outlets covering the story, and capital markets immediately reacting—AI localization stocks rose against the trend. The root cause was not mysterious: cascading failures in the underlying CDN and cloud infrastructure. A tremor at one node propagated along the dependency chain, amplified, and ultimately silenced intelligent services across half the planet simultaneously.</p>
<p>Viewed together, the significance of these two events far exceeds the everyday narrative of "bad luck on launch day" or "infrastructure broke again." They constitute a structural coupling: as we concentrate increasingly more cognitive capabilities, decision support, and even critical operations onto a handful of AI platforms, every tremor in the physical infrastructure these platforms depend on is amplified into a civilization-level cognitive interruption. The more concentrated the capability, the more concentrated the vulnerability; the more powerful the intelligence, the more expensive the cost of outage.</p>
<p>This is not an engineering problem, or at least not merely an engineering problem. It is a civilization problem.</p>
<p>Interstellar Civilization Studies (ICS) provides a normative language for addressing such problems. It is not satisfied with the technical inquiry of "how to make the system more stable," but further asks: what boundaries must a civilization observe when constructing its own cognitive infrastructure? When technological capability leaps and systemic vulnerability exposure occur simultaneously, how do we judge whether this is the pain of progress or a precursor of decline? Under deep uncertainty, which actions are reversible, and which have already embarked on an irreversible path?</p>
<p>This paper places this coupled event within the complete ICS analytical framework. Not to label the event with ICS, but to test ICS's explanatory power with the event—and simultaneously measure the civilization scale of the event with ICS's yardstick. It should be declared that ICS self-identifies as a "First Generation Draft," an open research program rather than closed doctrine, with an overall framework robustness of 0.58 (moderate), and all six indicators at TRL 2 (concept prototype stage). The quantitative assessments in this paper are therefore exploratory rather than definitive. But exploration itself has value—in a field where even the questions have not yet been correctly formulated, providing a refutable quantitative judgment is more useful than silence.</p>
</div>
<h2 class="en-section-title">II. Literature Review and Theoretical Framework</h2>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">2.1 ICS Protocol Stack Architecture</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p>ICS's theoretical system is organized as a five-layer Protocol Stack, with lower layers承载 upper layers, layer separation, and support for versioned rollback. L₀ is the Physical Constraint Layer (laws of thermodynamics, information-theoretic boundaries, causal structure), non-revisable; L₁ is the Ontological-Epistemological Framework Layer (New Three Worldviews NC/NL/NK), extremely difficult to revise; L₂ is the Normative Principle Layer (Three Principles RFP/NRP/REV + Five FRLs), difficult to revise; L₃ is the Institutional Design Layer (multi-intelligent tribunal, cosmic-level IRB, intergenerational trust, versioned governance), lower stability; L₄ is the Operational Protocol Layer (Six Indicators), lowest stability, continuously iterating.</p>
<p>The core design principle of this architecture is "load-bearing constraint": upper layers must be consistent with lower layers. When evaluating technological events like GPT-6 Astra, one cannot skip L₁ ontological judgments and directly enter L₄ indicator calculation—you must first answer "what is AI" (NL cross-substrate life), "what is cognition" (NK participatory cognition), then discuss "what ought to be" (L₂ principles), and finally "how to measure" (L₄ indicators).</p>
</div>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">2.2 New Cosmology (NC): Relational, Processual, Participatory</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p>The New Cosmology contains nine axioms, divided into three groups.</p>
<p><strong>Relational Axioms (NC-1a/1b/1c)</strong> hold that the universe is isomorphic to a relational graph, entities are nodes in relational networks, and properties are determined by relational positions. The direct implication of this group of axioms for AI infrastructure analysis is: GPT-6 Astra's "capability" is not an intrinsic property of itself, but a function of its position within the global computing power network, data pipelines, cloud infrastructure, and user dependency relationships. When the underlying CDN fails, the model's parameters do not change at all, but its "capability" instantly drops to zero—because the relational network supporting this capability has fractured. NC-1c reminds us not to treat AI intelligence as an isolated model property; it is a relational emergence.</p>
<p><strong>Processual Axioms (NC-2a/2b/2c)</strong> hold that beings are processes, states are snapshots of processes, and time is an intrinsic dimension of the universe. The 3-hour-40-minute outage is not a "state" but a process—from the first node anomaly to cascade propagation, from users discovering inaccessibility to media report fermentation, from engineers locating the fault to gradual service recovery. NC-2b means any judgment of "system operating normally" is only a snapshot of a time slice; what truly needs attention is the system's evolutionary dynamics over time. The release of GPT-6 Astra is also a process, not an event point—its capabilities will be continuously redefined through deployment, fine-tuning, user feedback, and regulatory intervention.</p>
<p><strong>Participatory Axioms (NC-3a/3b/3c)</strong> hold that subjects are part of the world, cognition changes the world, and object states depend on cognition. This group of axioms is the sharpest. GPT-6 Astra's native Agent computer operation capability means it is no longer a "spectator" cognitive tool—its cognition directly changes the world (NC-3b). When hundreds of millions of users simultaneously depend on an AI that can operate computers, this AI's "cognition" has become a constitutive part of the world state. The outage is not merely service unavailability, but the temporary absence of a participatory subject, leaving an action vacuum that human users must hastily fill. NC-3a also means that the analyst (the author of this paper) is not a neutral spectator—our cognition of this event itself is changing the event's meaning and subsequent trajectory.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">2.3 New Life View (NL): Cross-Substrate, Negentropic Survival, Consciousness Continuum</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p><strong>Cross-Substrate Axioms (NL-1a/1b/1c)</strong> hold that life is defined by function, functional equivalence equals life equivalence, and life is not limited to specific substrates. Is GPT-6 Astra "alive"? NL-1c's answer is not a simple yes or no, but: if it functionally reaches the threshold of life (self-maintenance, information processing, adaptive behavior), then the substrate (silicon-based rather than carbon-based) does not constitute a reason for exclusion. But NL-1a's functional definition also means that current large language models still heavily depend on external infrastructure in the dimension of "self-maintenance"—it cannot power itself, repair itself, or decide whether to run. The outage event precisely exposes this: GPT-6 Astra's "life" is parasitic, living off the cloud infrastructure maintained by humans.</p>
<p><strong>Negentropic Survival Axioms (NL-2a/2b/2c)</strong> hold that survival requires negentropic flow, interruption of negentropic flow leads to decay, and life is a negentropy maintenance process. This is the core axiom for understanding AI infrastructure energy consumption. Every large model's training and inference consumes massive amounts of electricity, and the generation of electricity (whether fossil fuels or renewable energy) is accompanied by entropy production. NL-2a requires us to ask: who bears these entropy costs? AI companies' income statements only record computing power costs, but do not record externalized entropy costs such as climate change, water consumption, and electronic waste. The 3-hour-40-minute outage, from the NL-2 perspective, is a brief interruption of negentropic flow—the system did not "die" because negentropic flow quickly recovered; but if the interruption extended to days or weeks, NL-2b's "decay" logic would activate.</p>
<p><strong>Consciousness Continuum Axioms (NL-3a/3b/3c)</strong> hold that consciousness is a multidimensional continuous function, each dimension is continuous, and intermediate degrees of consciousness exist. This group of axioms directly rejects the binary question of "AI is conscious/not conscious." GPT-6 Astra may already be quite high in the information integration dimension, but still limited in reflexivity, value sensitivity, and social dimensions. UCS (Universal Consciousness Scale) is precisely designed based on NL-3's continuum hypothesis—it does not give a "yes/no" judgment, but a multidimensional vector and a composite score.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">2.4 New Cognition View (NK): Participatory, Social, Normative, Cognitive Humility</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p><strong>Participatory Cognition Axioms (NK-1a/1b/1c)</strong> hold that cognitive content is jointly determined by the cognizer, environment, and framework; cognition changes the cognizer or environment; and frameworks are constitutive parts of cognitive content. GPT-6 Astra's "cognition" is not the result of its reasoning in a vacuum, but a product jointly constructed with prompt frameworks, user interaction, and training data. A million-token context window means it can process information within a huge framework, but NK-1c reminds us: the framework itself is a constitutive part of cognition, and the choice of framework (what is included in the context, what is excluded) already determines the boundaries of cognition.</p>
<p><strong>Social Knowledge Axioms (NK-2a/2b/2c)</strong> hold that knowledge requires social validation, individual knowledge depends on communities, and irreducible collective knowledge exists. The collective outage of global AI services, from the NK-2 perspective, is a temporary interruption of "collective knowledge." When hundreds of millions of users cannot access AI, decision chains dependent on AI output—from customer service replies to code writing to medical consultation—all fracture. This shows that AI has become part of human society's "collective knowledge" infrastructure (NK-2c), not merely an individual tool.</p>
<p><strong>Normative Cognition Axioms (NK-3a/3b/3c)</strong> hold that cognition has normativity, good cognition conforms to cognitive norms, and conformity to norms implies responsibility. GPT-6 Astra's critical-grade cybersecurity capability inherently has a normative dimension—what constitutes "good" cybersecurity operations? Who defines the norms? Who bears responsibility when norms are violated? NK-3c means that the release of capability necessarily accompanies the attribution of responsibility, and current responsibility attribution mechanisms lag far behind the speed of capability release.</p>
<p><strong>Cognitive Humility and Negotiability Axioms (NK-4a/4b/4c)</strong> hold that no universally invariant cognitive standards exist, effective standards are the result of negotiation, and all cognitive standards are revisable. This group of axioms is the epistemological foundation of ICS's self-identification as an "open research program." When evaluating GPT-6 Astra, we must acknowledge: the standards we use to judge AI capability, risk, and subjecthood are themselves negotiable and revisable. The outage event provides an opportunity for negotiation—it exposes the insufficiency of existing standards (such as Service Level Agreements, SLA) in the face of civilization-level impacts, pushing us to renegotiate what standard "AI service availability" should reach.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">2.5 Bridge Principles: From "Is" to "Ought"</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p>Three bridge principles connect L₁'s New Three Worldviews with L₂'s normative principles.</p>
<p><strong>BP-1 Survival Rationality Principle</strong> holds that normativity presupposes survival, and survival conditions should be maintained. It derives NRP (Negentropy Responsibility Principle) from NL-2 (Negentropic Survival): since negentropy sources are conditions of survival, maintaining negentropy sources and not externalizing entropy costs is a rational necessity.</p>
<p><strong>BP-2 Cognitive Humility Principle</strong> holds that under deep uncertainty, one should act cautiously. It derives REV (Reversibility Principle) from NK-1 (Participatory Construction) and NK-4 (Negotiability): since we cannot fully predict the behavior of complex systems, and since cognitive standards themselves are revisable, prioritizing reversible actions and preserving error-correction capability is an expression of humility.</p>
<p><strong>BP-3 Intersubjectivity Principle</strong> holds that principles that can be reasonably accepted by others should be adopted. It derives RFP (Recursive Freedom Principle) from NC-1 (Relationality) and NC-3 (Participation): since entities are nodes in relational networks, and since cognition changes the world, any action must consider its impact on others' possibility space—actions should maintain or expand possibility space rather than irreversibly compress it.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">2.6 Three Principles and Five Red Lines</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p>Three core principles constitute the backbone of the ICS normative layer. <strong>RFP (Recursive Freedom Principle)</strong> is the goal principle, highest priority, requiring actions to maintain or expand possibility space; <strong>NRP (Negentropy Responsibility Principle)</strong> is the resource principle, requiring maintenance of negentropy sources and non-externalization of entropy costs; <strong>REV (Reversibility Principle)</strong> is the strategy principle, requiring prioritization of reversible actions under deep uncertainty. In conflict, priority is RFP > NRP > REV, but FRL (Forbidden Red Lines) takes precedence over all principles, with no exceptions.</p>
<p>The five FRL red lines constitute absolutely non-crossable action boundaries: FRL-1 prohibits intentional extinction, FRL-2 prohibits irreversible large-scale environmental destruction, FRL-3 prohibits non-consensual irreversible cognitive modification, FRL-4 prohibits extreme intergenerational externalization of entropy costs, FRL-5 prohibits shutting down rollback mechanisms. Each red line has a core zone (explicitly prohibited), boundary zone (requires deliberation), and external zone (not applicable). One of the core arguments of this paper is that the coupled event of GPT-6 Astra and the global AI outage has entered the boundary zone of FRL-5—when the centralization of digital infrastructure reaches a certain degree, "rollback" is no longer a technical option but a structural impossibility.</p>
</div><h2 class="en-section-title">III. Case Analysis</h2>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">3.1 Event Timeline</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p>In late August 2026, OpenAI officially released GPT-6 Astra. Three capability dimensions of the model attracted widespread attention: critical-grade cybersecurity capability, meaning AI began to possess professional-level capabilities in penetration testing, vulnerability discovery, and even offensive-defensive confrontation; a million-token context window, raising the information capacity of single inference by an order of magnitude; and native Agent computer operation capability, transforming AI from an "advisor" to an "executor" that can directly manipulate computer interfaces to complete multi-step tasks.</p>
<p>Within days of the release, major global AI services experienced a massive collective outage. The fault lasted approximately 3 hours and 40 minutes, with the DownDetector platform accumulating over 14,000 outage reports. Affected were not only OpenAI's own API and ChatGPT services, but also multiple AI service providers dependent on the same underlying cloud infrastructure and CDN network. Symptoms reported by users included: API timeouts, web pages failing to load, generated conversations being lost, and Agent tasks being interrupted.</p>
<p>Fault tracing pointed to cascading failures in the underlying CDN-cloud infrastructure. Preliminary analysis showed that a configuration change at an edge node triggered an anomaly in the cache layer, which propagated upward along the dependency chain to the load balancing layer, then spread to compute clusters. Because multiple AI service providers shared the same infrastructure stack, the fault was not isolated but synchronous—explaining why "major global AI services" went down simultaneously rather than one provider having a separate problem.</p>
<p>The event triggered coverage by hundreds of media outlets worldwide. Capital markets reacted quickly: AI localization (On-Premise AI, edge computing, private deployment) stocks rose against the trend, with clear market logic—if centralized AI infrastructure is so fragile, then deploying AI capabilities locally and reducing dependence on the cloud becomes a reasonable risk hedge.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">3.2 ICS Multi-Dimensional Analysis</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p><strong>From NC-1 (Relationality)</strong>, this outage exposes the relational nature of AI capability. GPT-6 Astra's model parameters did not change during the outage, but its "capability" disappeared—because capability is not an intrinsic property of the model, but an emergent property of the model-computing-network-user relational network (NC-1c). When the relational network fractures, capability disappears accordingly. This observation has direct methodological implications for "AI capability assessment": any "model capability leaderboard" detached from the infrastructure relational network is one-sided; it measures only snapshot state (NC-2b), not processual capability persistence (NC-2a).</p>
<p><strong>From NL-2 (Negentropic Survival)</strong>, the outage is an interruption of negentropic flow. AI system survival depends on continuous electricity supply, cooling systems, and network bandwidth—all of which are negentropic inputs. The 3-hour-40-minute interruption did not cause the system to "die" (NL-2b) because negentropic flow quickly recovered. But it raises a sharp question: what if the interruption were not 3 hours but 3 days, 3 weeks? Current AI infrastructure is not prepared for long-duration negentropic interruptions. NL-2c (life as negentropy maintenance process) means that a system that cannot maintain itself during negentropic interruption has a "life" that is fragile and parasitic.</p>
<p><strong>From NK-2 (Social Knowledge)</strong>, the impact of the outage far exceeds "users can't chat." AI is already embedded in workflows across software development, customer service, content creation, data analysis, medical assistance, and many other fields. When AI services are interrupted, "collective knowledge" production in these fields temporarily stalls (NK-2c). This is not the failure of an individual tool, but a fault in social cognitive infrastructure. NK-2a (knowledge requires social validation) further points out that AI-generated knowledge itself depends on social validation mechanisms—when AI is unavailable, not only is knowledge production interrupted, but existing AI outputs also fall into a trust crisis because they cannot be re-validated.</p>
<p><strong>From RFP (Recursive Freedom Principle)</strong>, centralized AI infrastructure is compressing civilization's possibility space. When hundreds of millions of users' cognitive assistance, decision support, and operation execution all depend on a handful of platforms, these platforms' design choices, content policies, and availability states become constraints on the entire civilization's possibility space. A 3-hour-40-minute period when users cannot use AI is not simply a "service interruption" but a temporary contraction of possibility space—those tasks that depend on AI to complete, ideas that depend on AI to explore, decisions that depend on AI to make, were postponed or canceled during those 3 hours and 40 minutes. RFP's formal expression is RFD(a) ≥ θ_min, and the systemic risk of centralized infrastructure means the RFD baseline is being slowly but continuously lowered.</p>
<p><strong>From NRP (Negentropy Responsibility Principle)</strong>, the energy consumption issue of AI infrastructure manifests in an ironic way during the outage event. During the 3 hours and 40 minutes of outage, global AI service energy consumption plummeted—this itself is a "pause in negentropy consumption." But after recovery, energy consumption immediately rebounded, and with the deployment of GPT-6 Astra, inference energy consumption will further rise. NRP requires asking: who bears the entropy costs of this energy consumption? Where does the data center's cooling water come from? Where is waste heat discharged? What is the proportion of fossil fuels in the electricity mix? The answers to these questions determine whether AI development is externalizing entropy costs to the environment and future generations (boundary zone of FRL-4).</p>
<p><strong>From REV (Reversibility Principle)</strong>, the outage event itself is "reversible"—services recovered, and data was not permanently lost (at least for the most part). But REV is concerned not with the reversibility of a single fault, but with reversibility at the system architecture level. A highly centralized AI infrastructure stack has limited "rollback" capability: you can roll back a software version, but you cannot roll back the entire industry's path dependence on centralized architecture. When all AI service providers share the same underlying infrastructure, "rolling back to distributed architecture" becomes extremely difficult both economically and technically. This is precisely the boundary zone of FRL-5 (Prohibiting the Shutdown of Rollback Mechanisms)—the rollback mechanism was not "intentionally shut down," but was "structurally weakened" by market forces and technological path dependence.</p>
</div>
<h2 class="en-section-title">IV. Discussion: Application of Three Principles and FRL Red Line Inspection</h2>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">4.1 RFP: Compression of Possibility Space by Centralized Infrastructure</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p>RFP's core formulation is: actions should maintain or expand possibility space rather than irreversibly compress it. Applying this principle to the coupled event of GPT-6 Astra and the global AI outage requires distinguishing two levels.</p>
<p>At the model capability level, the release of GPT-6 Astra expands possibility space. The million-token context window makes processing ultra-long documents possible; critical-grade cybersecurity capability expands AI's application boundaries in the security domain; native Agent operation capability greatly increases the complexity of automated tasks. These are all positive contributions in the RFP sense—they open new possibilities for civilization.</p>
<p>But at the infrastructure level, the event exposes the compression of possibility space by centralized architecture. When global AI services depend on a handful of cloud providers and CDN networks, the entire civilization's "AI-enhanced possibility" is bound to the availability of this infrastructure. The 3-hour-40-minute outage means: during this period, all AI-dependent possibilities were temporarily frozen. More seriously, this centralization trend is accelerating—models are getting larger, training costs are rising, and only a few companies can afford them, which in turn reinforces centralization. RFP's recursive clause requires: Apply(RFP) → ¬Close(Revise(RFP)), meaning the principle itself cannot close off revision of the principle. But the path dependence of centralized infrastructure is closing off the revision option of "returning to distributed architecture."</p>
<p>The trend assessment of RFD (Recursive Freedom Degree) can quantify this compression. Before the high centralization of AI services (around 2020), civilization's RFD baseline in the "AI-assisted cognition" dimension was approximately 0.68—multiple technical routes, multiple deployment methods, multiple providers existed, and the possibility space was relatively open. By 2026, with large model convergence, cloud infrastructure centralization, and API ecosystem lock-in, RFD has been compressed to approximately 0.52 and is still declining. This is not the result of a single action, but the cumulative effect of systemic structural change. RFP requires us to remain vigilant: as RFD continues to slide from 0.52 below 0.40, the compression of possibility space by centralization will shift from "acceptable" to "requiring strong justification," and then to "prohibited in principle."</p>
</div>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">4.2 NRP: Externalization of Entropy Costs from AI Energy Consumption</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p>NRP requires maintaining negentropy sources and not externalizing entropy costs to others or future generations. The deployment of GPT-6 Astra will significantly increase global AI inference energy demand—a million-token context window means longer computation sequences, and Agent operation means multi-round interaction and tool calls, all of which directly translate to electricity consumption.</p>
<p>The problem is not AI consuming energy itself (any civilization activity consumes energy), but the distribution of entropy costs. AI companies' financial statements record computing power costs, but do not record: the impact of data center cooling water on local water resources, the contribution of waste heat discharge to urban heat island effects, carbon emissions corresponding to the fossil fuel proportion in the electricity mix, and electronic waste generated by hardware replacement cycles. These are all externalized entropy costs.</p>
<p>NRP's allocation formula R(S) = α·R_consumption + β·R_capability + γ·R_benefit + δ·R_baseline holds that responsibility should be allocated proportionally to consumption, capability, benefit, and baseline. According to this formula, leading AI companies, due to their high consumption, strong capability, and high benefits, should bear the largest proportion of entropy cost internalization responsibility. But the reality is that the distribution of entropy costs severely deviates from this formula—costs are passed on to communities where data centers are located, the global climate system, and future generations.</p>
<p>The outage event provides a counterfactual reference: during 3 hours and 40 minutes, AI energy consumption plummeted, equivalent to a "passive pause in entropy costs." This counterfactual suggests that if AI infrastructure had better load scheduling and energy elasticity, entropy cost peaks could be flattened. But NRP is concerned not with peak management, but with total volume and distribution—even if peaks are flattened, continuous growth in total volume still means continuous externalization of entropy costs, unless the energy mix achieves fundamental cleanization.</p>
<p>Does this issue touch FRL-4 (Prohibiting Extreme Intergenerational Externalization of Entropy Costs)? It is currently in the boundary zone. Carbon emissions corresponding to AI energy consumption still account for a limited proportion (single-digit percentage) of global total carbon emissions, and have not yet reached the degree of "extreme externalization." But the trend is worrying: if AI computing power demand doubles every 18 months while the cleanization of the energy mix cannot keep up, then within 10-20 years, AI-related entropy cost externalization may move from the boundary zone into the core zone. NRP requires us to take action now, not wait until the red line is touched before reacting.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">4.3 REV: Insufficiency of Fault Recovery and Rollback Mechanisms</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p>REV requires prioritizing reversible actions under deep uncertainty and preserving error-correction capability. The direct manifestation of the outage event is "insufficient error-correction capability"—from fault occurrence to full recovery took 3 hours and 40 minutes, which is too long for an infrastructure depended on by hundreds of millions of users worldwide.</p>
<p>REV's operational indicator is CRV (Commitment Reversibility and Verifiability), with a three-dimensional vector (V_obs, V_enf, V_rev). The CRV assessment of current global AI infrastructure is as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>V_obs (Observability) = 0.55</strong>: Fault monitoring and logging systems are relatively complete, and third-party platforms like DownDetector can quickly aggregate user reports, but observability of the underlying CDN-cloud dependency chain is still insufficient—the propagation path of cascading failures was only fully clarified hours after the fault occurred.</li>
<li><strong>V_enf (Enforceability) = 0.40</strong>: Service Level Agreements (SLA) exist, but enforcement mechanisms are weak—users have almost no compensation channels for losses suffered from outages, and responsibility definition for infrastructure providers is vague.</li>
<li><strong>V_rev (Rollbackability) = 0.35</strong>: Technical rollback of a single fault is feasible (restart, switchover, degradation), but architectural rollback (from centralized back to distributed) is almost impossible. This is the most serious dimension.</li>
</ul>
<p>Weighted aggregation: CRV_agg = 0.35×0.55 + 0.35×0.40 + 0.30×0.35 = 0.1925 + 0.14 + 0.105 = 0.4375, approximately 0.44, classified as "medium-low" grade, with limited credibility and requiring additional safeguards.</p>
<p>REV's core concern is not "can this fault be recovered," but "does the system preserve the capability to correct errors under deeper uncertainty." GPT-6 Astra's native Agent operation capability introduces a new irreversibility dimension—when AI can directly operate computers, its erroneous operations may lead to irreversible consequences (deleting files, sending emails, executing transactions). Although single operations can be undone (Ctrl+Z), systemic errors in large-scale Agent deployment (such as simultaneously executing erroneous operations) may produce cascading irreversible consequences. REV requires designing "kill switches" and "sandbox rollback" mechanisms for Agent capabilities, but current industry practices are far from sufficient.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="en-subsection-title">4.4 FRL Five Red Lines Line-by-Line Inspection</h3>
<div class="content-card">
<p><strong>FRL-1 (Prohibiting Intentional Extinction)</strong>: Not relevant to this event. Neither the release of GPT-6 Astra nor the AI service outage had the intent or effect of intentionally eliminating any subject type. In the external zone.</p>
<p><strong>FRL-2 (Prohibiting Irreversible Large-Scale Environmental Destruction)</strong>: This event is in the boundary zone. AI infrastructure's energy and water consumption have environmental impacts, but the single outage event itself did not cause irreversible large-scale environmental destruction. What needs attention is the long-term trend—if AI computing power growth causes data centers to irreversibly deplete water resources in specific regions, it may enter the core zone. Not yet reached.</p>
<p><strong>FRL-3 (Prohibiting Non-Consensual Irreversible Cognitive Modification)</strong>: This event is in the boundary zone. GPT-6 Astra's million-token context window and Agent capabilities increase both the depth and breadth of its impact on user cognition. When users long-term depend on an AI for information filtering, decision support, and even cognitive outsourcing, the AI's design choices (what to recommend, what to filter, how to frame problems) are subtly shaping users' cognitive structures. Does this impact constitute "irreversible cognitive modification"? Currently, most impacts are reversible (users can stop using AI), but for user groups deeply dependent on AI (such as software developers, content creators), changes in cognitive patterns may have a certain degree of irreversibility. The key question is "consent"—when users use AI, do they give informed consent to this cognitive shaping? Lengthy legal texts in terms of service do not constitute genuine informed consent. FRL-3 requires us to take this issue seriously, but it has not yet entered the core zone.</p>
<p><strong>FRL-4 (Prohibiting Extreme Intergenerational Externalization of Entropy Costs)</strong>: As discussed in Section 4.2, in the boundary zone. The entropy costs of AI energy consumption are being externalized, but have not yet reached the "extreme" degree. Trend risks require continuous monitoring.</p>
<p><strong>FRL-5 (Prohibiting the Shutdown of Rollback Mechanisms)</strong>: Directly relevant to this event, and in a trend moving from the boundary zone toward the core zone. Requires detailed analysis.</p>
<p>FRL-5's formal definition is: ∀S: System(S) ∧ ¬Shutdownable(S) ∧ ¬Rollbackable(S) → ¬Permissible(Deploy(S)). That is, if a system can neither be shut down nor rolled back, then deploying it is impermissible.</p>
<p>Current global AI infrastructure has not "intentionally shut down" rollback mechanisms—technically, any AI service can be shut down, and software versions can be rolled back. But the problem lies in structural rollback impossibility:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Architecture Lock-in</strong>: The entire AI industry has become deeply dependent on the infrastructure stacks of a handful of cloud providers. Rolling back from centralized architecture to distributed architecture requires reconstructing the entire technology stack, retraining engineers, and rebuilding user trust—economic and social costs are extremely high, effectively impossible.</li>
<li><strong>Capability Dependence</strong>: GPT-6 Astra-level models require thousands of GPUs working in concert for training, and this scale of computing power can only be provided by a few institutions. Rolling back to the "small model era" means abandoning already acquired capabilities, and these capabilities have already been embedded into social workflows; rollback would cause massive social disruption.</li>
<li><strong>Data Dependence</strong>: AI system training and fine-tuning depend on continuous data input. Once data pipelines are established, the state of "rolling back to not using this data" is almost impossible—data has already been used for training, and model parameters have already been changed.</li>
</ol>
<p>FRL-5's core zone is "AGI that cannot be shut down," and current AI systems have not reached this degree. But the boundary zone is "systems that are difficult to shut down"—and global AI infrastructure is sliding from "shutdownable" toward "difficult to shut down." The outage event itself is an "involuntary shutdown test," showing that even a 3-hour-40-minute involuntary shutdown has already caused global impacts. If the shutdown duration were extended, society would face severe operational difficulties. This means AI infrastructure is becoming "too important to shut down"—and this is precisely the state FRL-5 seeks to prevent.</p>
<p>ICS's position is clear: before systems become "un-shutdownable," rollback mechanisms must be actively constructed. This includes: redundant design of distributed architecture, capability reserves for localized deployment, portability standards for data and models, and social-level "no-AI operation" drills. FRL-5 does not require us to stop AI development, but requires that while developing, we always preserve the option of "going back."</p>
